course: dictionary
lesson: 225
title: All, in many shapes
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: «All» is a small word with many jobs. It can mean every one, the whole of something, or completely. The words around it tell you which job it is doing, so use the context clues you learned in the last lesson.
explain: «I wandered lonely as a cloud» is a simile, and the same kind of thinking helps with «all». In «all alone», all means completely. In «all of them», all means every one. In «How many in all?», all means the total when the parts are added together.
explain: Sometimes «all» means a score. In a match, «two all» means each side has two points, so the game is level. Casual talk can change it again: «she was all, "no way!"» means she said it, and y'all in the southern US means you all, every one of you.
fact: All means every one, the whole lot, with none left out.
fact: As an adverb, all means completely, as in «she was all wet».
fact: In sport, «two all» or «3–3» means each side has the same score.
fact: «Y'all» (US South) means you all, and «fixin' to» means about to.
fact: Formal writing may use words such as «in total», «collectively» or «the aggregate» where you would say «all».
exercise: «They ate all the cake.» What does all mean here? | *The whole of the cake | Nothing at all | Some of the cake, just one slice | Only the crumbs
exercise: At a football match, the score is «two all». What does it mean? | *Each side has the same score | The game has no goals | One side has won | Everyone has left the ground
exercise: Find and copy one word from «Think back over all these families» that means every one. | back | *all | families | these
test: «Some words don't change at all: one sheep, two sheep.» What does at all mean here? | Every one of them | *In any way, not even a little | Very loudly | In total, added together
test: Ruby says, «Y'all come over on Sunday!» What does y'all mean? | *You, every one of you | We, just the two of us | They, over there | I, on my own
exam: In «How many in all?», what does in all mean? | In a rush, very fast | With all the others, not left out | *In total, the whole amount when the parts are added | At the very start
